// BEHAVIOURAL guard for the Freeze predicate that gates `noalias`+`readonly`
// on a shared `&T` (rule `type.shared-ref.write-through-derived-ub`,
// src/compiler/mlir_gen_fn.cpp). A type with interior mutability (UnsafeCell,
// directly or transitively) must NOT be classified Freeze — otherwise the
// optimizer is told the callee never writes through the reference, and the
// read after the mutation folds back to the pre-mutation value.
//
// ── WHY THIS FILE LOOKS THE WAY IT DOES ─────────────────────────────────────
// It used to hold the `observe_*` functions ITSELF, and it asserted nothing.
// MEASURED 2026-08-04: with the Freeze gate forced open in the compiler
// (`if (true || type_is_freeze(...))`, so `&Cell<i64>` and `&Wrap` WRONGLY
// carry noalias+readonly — confirmed in the emitted IR), the old file still
// exited 0 at -O0, -O1, -O2 AND -O3. The reasoning was sound; the test was
// not. A parameter attribute is a claim made TO LLVM, and LLVM can only act on
// it while the call is still a call: in one translation unit `observe_cell`
// inlines into `main`, the parameter and its attributes disappear, and the
// optimizer simply sees the real stores.
//
// So the mutators moved ACROSS AN ARCHIVE BOUNDARY. They now live in
// tests/logos/ub_boundary/, which is compiled separately into libub_boundary.a
// AT -O2 (tests/logos/CMakeLists.txt). The read → mutate → read sequence has to
// survive optimization inside that archive, with the attribute still attached
// to a real definition, before this file ever links against it.
//
// CONTROL REVERT, both exit codes observed directly:
//   * HEAD                        → exit 0 (ctest green)
//   * Freeze gate forced open     → exit 1, i.e. ctest RED: the `observe_cell`
//                                   probe returns the STALE pre-mutation value.
//     The `observe_wrap` probe was proved to bite ON ITS OWN in a separate run
//     — a consumer holding only that probe exited 3 under the same revert, and
//     under the WEAKER condition of a stdlib still built by the good compiler.
//     So neither probe is carried by the other.
//   * gate restored               → exit 0
// The CONTROL (`bump_plain`, exit 5) stayed green through all of that, which is
// what makes the reds above mean "the predicate broke" rather than "the archive
// or the boundary broke".
// Note the bite does NOT depend on the -O level of THIS file: the miscompile is
// baked into the archive, so it fires at -O0 too. That is deliberate — the
// corpus compiles at logosc's default -O0.
package canary_interior_mut;

use logos.lang.cell;
use ub_boundary;

fn main() -> i32 {
    // ── THE CONTROL RUNS FIRST, and it must, or a green below could mean
    //    "the archive was never reached" instead of "the predicate is right".
    //    `&mut Counter` on a Freeze pointee takes the OTHER branch of the same
    //    emission (noalias from borrow-checker exclusivity, never readonly), so
    //    it exercises the identical cross-archive read → mutate → read at -O2
    //    while being untouched by the Freeze gate in either direction. If the
    //    boundary or the mutation path were dead, this fails too and the
    //    diagnosis is unambiguous.
    //
    //    ⚠ MEASURED, and it is why this is a Counter and not a Cell. The first
    //    version used `&mut Cell<i64>` — and it FIRED (exit 5) on the very
    //    revert the probes are supposed to catch, because `Cell::set` takes a
    //    SHARED `&self` and so the "control" ran straight through the branch it
    //    was controlling for. A control that shares the mechanism with its
    //    subject is not a control.
    let mut m: Counter = Counter { n: 1i64 };
    if bump_plain(&mut m) != 2i64 { return 5; }

    // ── PROBE 1: &Cell<i64>. UnsafeCell is the pointee's own struct.
    let c: Cell<i64> = Cell::new(5i64);
    if observe_cell(&c) != 105i64 { return 1; }
    if c.get() != 105i64 { return 2; }

    // ── PROBE 2: &Wrap. UnsafeCell reached through a plain struct FIELD —
    //    the transitive half of the predicate.
    let w: Wrap = Wrap { inner: Cell::new(10i64), tag: 0i64 };
    if observe_wrap(&w) != 17i64 { return 3; }
    if w.inner.get() != 17i64 { return 4; }

    return 0;
}
